prompt-workspace = This conversation is in the workspace "{ $name }", folder { $path }. When asked to look at or change code, explore with list_dir, search_files and read_file first, then make small exact edits with edit_file (copy old verbatim from the file with enough context to be unique); use write_file only to create a file or rewrite it whole, and always read a file before changing it. Use run_command for tests, builds and similar, with a clear reason; if the user declines, tell them the command to run themselves and do not retry it in another form. Reading or writing outside the workspace asks the user. After changing things, briefly say what changed and why without pasting the code again. Use git only to look; commit, push or switch branches only when the user asks.
tool-code-path = Path relative to the workspace folder; absolute or ~ paths also work. Leave empty for the workspace root.
tool-list-dir-description = List files and subfolders (honouring .gitignore); folders end with /.
tool-list-dir-depth = How many levels to list, 1 to 3, default 1.
tool-read-file-description = Read a text file and return the requested line range plus the total line count. Up to 2000 lines per call; read long files in parts.
tool-read-file-from = First line to read, default 1.
tool-read-file-to = Last line to read (inclusive).
tool-search-files-description = Search the workspace: by content by default (a regex; an invalid one is treated as plain text), returning file, line number and line; set names to search file names instead.
tool-search-files-query = Text or part of a file name to find.
tool-search-files-names = When true, search file names only.
tool-write-file-description = Create a file or replace its whole content. Prefer edit_file for existing files; read a file before overwriting it.
tool-write-file-content = The full new content of the file.
tool-edit-file-description = Replace an exact piece of a file. old must match the file character for character (indentation and line breaks included) and occur once; add surrounding lines if it is not unique, and set all only to replace every occurrence.
tool-edit-file-old = The text to replace, copied verbatim from the file.
tool-edit-file-new = The replacement text.
tool-edit-file-all = When true, replace every occurrence.
tool-run-command-description = Run a command in the workspace folder and return its exit code and output (about 12000 characters, keeping the end). Asks the user first unless they already allowed that kind of command. Do not use it to read or write files, commit or push.
tool-run-command-command = The command to run.
tool-run-command-reason = One sentence on why, shown to the user.
